
<section class="mt-10 border-t-2 border-orange-100 pt-8">
  <div class="mb-6 flex items-start gap-4">
    <div class="flex h-14 w-14 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-2xl border-2 border-orange-200 bg-orange-100 text-2xl">
      📚
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="text-base font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-orange-600">
        Administrator handbook
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-black text-slate-800 sm:text-3xl">
        How research workspaces work
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">
        A workspace is the persistent container for an investigation. It connects
        the research question, the objects being investigated, the notes and
        annotations collected during research, and the evidence used to evaluate findings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <article class="rounded-3xl border-2 border-orange-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-800">
        1. Workspace lifecycle
      </h3>

      <div class="mt-5 space-y-5">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-emerald-100 text-lg text-emerald-800">●</span>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Active</h4>
            <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
              Work is in progress. Researchers may still need to investigate
              questions, add items, evaluate evidence, or refine their conclusions.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-sky-100 text-lg text-sky-800">✓</span>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Completed</h4>
            <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
              The investigation has been marked as finished. Its status is a
              workflow decision, not an automatic guarantee that every claim has been verified.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-violet-100 text-lg text-violet-800">◼</span>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Archived</h4>
            <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
              The investigation has been set aside for reference. Check the
              application's restore and reopen actions before assuming it can
              be edited in the same way as an active workspace.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-3xl border-2 border-sky-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-800">
        2. Understanding research records
      </h3>

      <div class="mt-5 space-y-5">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Research items</h4>
          <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
            Individual units of work inside a workspace. Depending on the
            application's supported item types, these can represent entities,
            questions, or notes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Annotations</h4>
          <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
            Contextual explanations, observations, interpretations, or review
            notes associated with research. Review their status and authorship
            where those fields are available.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Evidence</h4>
          <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
            Records supporting or challenging a finding. Source details,
            confidence, and verification status help administrators assess
            how well a conclusion is supported.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800">Snapshots</h4>
          <p class="mt-1 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
            Saved representations of research state, when snapshot support is
            available. Inspect the snapshot and restore behavior before relying
            on it as a complete historical backup.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-3xl border-2 border-violet-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-800">
        3. Confidence and verification
      </h3>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        Confidence expresses how strongly a research record is assessed.
        Verification indicates that an evidence record has passed the
        application's verification process. These concepts are related,
        but they are not interchangeable.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-5 rounded-2xl border border-violet-100 bg-violet-50 p-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-7 text-violet-950">
          <strong>Good practice:</strong> Prefer traceable sources, document
          uncertainty, distinguish observations from interpretations, and
          record why a claim is considered reliable. A numerical confidence
          score should not replace critical review.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-3xl border-2 border-emerald-100 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-800">
        4. When records are missing
      </h3>

      <ol class="mt-4 list-inside list-decimal space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        <li>Check whether the current filter hides the investigation.</li>
        <li>Confirm that the investigation was successfully saved.</li>
        <li>Check which owner or visitor scope the controller applies.</li>
        <li>Inspect the workspace creation flow and its redirect destination.</li>
        <li>Check application logs for failed queries, authorization issues, or template errors.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        The library can only display the records made available to it by the
        controller. A polished empty state cannot correct missing or
        incorrectly scoped database records by itself.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-7 rounded-[26px] border-2 border-orange-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-50 via-white to-emerald-50 p-6">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-slate-800">
      Recommended investigation routine
    </h3>

    <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-orange-100 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="text-lg font-black text-orange-700">Discover</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          Define the problem and identify the entities or questions to investigate.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-sky-100 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="text-lg font-black text-sky-700">Investigate</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          Collect relevant information and record observations as research items.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-violet-100 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="text-lg font-black text-violet-700">Evaluate</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          Examine sources, annotate findings, and verify evidence where appropriate.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-emerald-100 bg-white p-4">
        <p class="text-lg font-black text-emerald-700">Preserve</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          Save the work, review the findings, and manage the workspace lifecycle.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>